WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.460
A salmon swims upriver, spawns once, and dies.

2
00:00:07.800 --> 00:00:09.920
A songbird nests every spring for years.

3
00:00:11.260 --> 00:00:13.080
Both are shaped by natural selection.

4
00:00:14.360 --> 00:00:16.300
So how can both strategies be right?

5
00:00:17.283 --> 00:00:19.403
Start with one bird in one season.

6
00:00:20.843 --> 00:00:23.743
Treat what it takes in and can use as one fixed budget.

7
00:00:24.963 --> 00:00:26.803
That budget must pay for four uses.

8
00:00:29.983 --> 00:00:31.943
Upkeep, meaning maintenance and repair.

9
00:00:33.423 --> 00:00:35.943
Storage. And reproduction.

10
00:00:37.233 --> 00:00:40.133
Lump the first three together as self, in teal.

11
00:00:41.513 --> 00:00:42.813
Reproduction stays gold.

12
00:00:44.133 --> 00:00:47.633
For this one bird, gold plus teal always equals the intake.

13
00:00:48.833 --> 00:00:50.493
So more gold means less teal.

14
00:00:51.673 --> 00:00:53.533
That negative link is a trade-off.

15
00:00:55.617 --> 00:00:57.917
Two classic trade-offs live on this line.

16
00:00:59.537 --> 00:01:04.057
First, current against future reproduction: what a parent spends on this year's

17
00:01:04.097 --> 00:01:05.477
young is not spent on itself.

18
00:01:06.837 --> 00:01:08.877
Second, number against size of offspring.

19
00:01:10.097 --> 00:01:14.737
A fixed amount put into a clutch can make many small eggs, or a few large ones.

20
00:01:16.050 --> 00:01:18.290
Picture two birds with the same intake.

21
00:01:19.570 --> 00:01:21.890
One pours most of it into this year's brood.

22
00:01:23.130 --> 00:01:24.670
The other keeps more for itself.

23
00:01:26.010 --> 00:01:28.390
Each sits at one point on the line, its split.

24
00:01:30.010 --> 00:01:31.230
Selection acts on that split.

25
00:01:32.450 --> 00:01:35.250
It favours whichever split leaves the most descendants.

26
00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:39.290
Which split wins depends on who dies at what age.

27
00:01:40.333 --> 00:01:41.693
Picture two worlds.

28
00:01:43.013 --> 00:01:47.453
In the first, adults rarely survive the winter, but their young survive well.

29
00:01:48.773 --> 00:01:53.633
In the second, adults usually survive, while many young die, or their survival

30
00:01:53.633 --> 00:01:55.053
swings from year to year.

31
00:01:56.117 --> 00:01:58.057
Here is a question for you.

32
00:01:59.037 --> 00:02:02.077
In the first world, adults rarely live to breed again.

33
00:02:03.377 --> 00:02:06.857
Does that lower or raise the extra young one big bout needs to pay?

34
00:02:13.333 --> 00:02:18.173
It lowers it. Holding back buys a future that rarely comes.

35
00:02:19.433 --> 00:02:22.853
So putting more into this year's bout can pay, as in the salmon.

36
00:02:24.213 --> 00:02:28.333
How far still depends on how extra effort changes breeding and survival.

37
00:02:29.733 --> 00:02:32.693
In the second world, teal buys more breeding seasons.

38
00:02:34.053 --> 00:02:37.133
Holding back to breed again can pay, as in the songbird.

39
00:02:38.217 --> 00:02:44.177
That is why no organism can grow fast, breed early, make many large offspring and

40
00:02:44.177 --> 00:02:45.357
live long all at once.

41
00:02:46.777 --> 00:02:49.617
Salmon and songbird follow one rule in different worlds.

42
00:02:50.837 --> 00:02:52.297
One intake, one split.

43
00:02:54.097 --> 00:02:58.177
Selection picks the split that leaves the most descendants where that animal lives.

44
00:02:59.267 --> 00:03:00.507
There is one catch.

45
00:03:01.847 --> 00:03:03.907
So far every bird had the same intake.

46
00:03:05.207 --> 00:03:07.847
Real animals do not all take in the same amount.

47
00:03:09.107 --> 00:03:12.087
Hold that thought, because it changes what real data show.
